  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how sex differences arise from both chromosomal effects and steroid hormone signaling, linking gonadal development to downstream cellular responses. At the top, sex chromosomes are associated with ovaries or testis, and steroid precursors such as DHEA lead to testosterone, which can be converted by aromatase into 17β-oestradiol. These hormones then act on a target cell through several routes.\n\nInside the cell, testosterone signals through the androgen receptor (AR), while oestradiol acts through estrogen receptors (ER, including ERα/β). The diagram emphasizes both genomic actions, where receptors and coregulators influence nuclear DNA and mRNA production, and non-genomic actions at or near the cell membrane. Membrane-associated receptors, including GPR30 and ERα, appear to activate signaling pathways such as PI3K–AKT, MAP kinase, and tyrosine kinase, as well as eNOS and nitric oxide production. Ion fluxes involving potassium and calcium are also shown as part of rapid signaling.\n\nThese pathways converge on protein synthesis and broader cell functions like metabolism, apoptosis, migration, and proliferation. Overall, the figure highlights that some hormone effects are rapid and reversible, whereas developmental or structural effects tied to gonadal differentiation are more permanent.",
